ors and patterns now seems critical.\n\n\n**Identifying the Inversion**\n\nI've made a significant leap. Analyzing Train 2 and Train 4, I see the pattern inside the frame is flipped horizontally relative to the frame's color. For example, when the frame is 9 on the left and 7 on the right, the pattern has 7 on the left and 9 on the right. This seems to provide a key link to the test input data.\n\n\n**Confirming the Correlation**\n\nI've made a crucial observation. Now, by examining a 2x6 grid within the input data, I've confirmed a direct correlation between the color arrangement of the frame and the numerical pattern inside. The data is either horizontally flipped to match the frame colors, or rotated 180 degrees. This confirms that the critical rows in the input data align with the frame's color arrangement. This also seems to be the crucial piece I was missing. I'm now certain I'm on the right track.\n\n\n**Verifying the Flip**\n\nI've just run a final check on Train 3, and confirmed a key rule. There's no horizontal flip when the pattern colors match the frame's colors. In every train examined, the pattern inside the frame is oriented so its colors match the colors of the frame itself. The frame's left/right colors directly dictate the pattern's left/right positioning. It is a reflection along the vertical axis (a horizontal flip), and not a rotation. This fully explains the inversion I was seeing before! I'm ready to move on.\n\n\n",
